### JobKeep — every route, in the order you would actually use them.
#
# Works in Visual Studio, VS Code (REST Client extension) and Rider with no account
# and no external service. Committed on purpose: it is version-controlled next to the
# routes it documents, so it moves in the same commit when a route moves.
#
# Start Postgres and the app first:
#   docker run -d -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=dev -e POSTGRES_DB=jobkeep postgres:16-alpine
#   cd src && dotnet run

@host = http://localhost:5080

### Create an application. Company and posting are created or reused by name.
# @name create
POST {{host}}/applications
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "company": "Canva",
  "title": "Backend Engineer",
  "location": "Melbourne",
  "description": "Build and operate .NET services on AWS.",
  "sourceUrl": "https://example.com/jobs/123",
  "notes": "Applied via referral"
}

### Capture the id from the response above for everything below.
@applicationId = {{create.response.body.id}}

### List: newest first, page 1, 20 rows. Every parameter below is optional.
GET {{host}}/applications

### Filter by status. The list is a page now: { items, totalCount, page, pageSize, totalPages }.
GET {{host}}/applications?status=Interviewing

### Company and title match ANYWHERE and case-insensitively (Postgres ILIKE).
GET {{host}}/applications?company=canva&title=engineer

### Skill matches the WHOLE name, case-insensitively — "C" must not return C# and C++.
### This one filter is the phase-2 storage argument: an EXISTS through posting_skills
### into the shared skills table, versus scanning every document in a NoSQL store.
GET {{host}}/applications?skill=C%23

### Applied-date range, inclusive at both ends.
GET {{host}}/applications?appliedFrom=2026-01-01&appliedTo=2026-12-31

### Sort and page. sort = DateApplied | Company | Title | Status | UpdatedAt.
GET {{host}}/applications?sort=Company&direction=Asc&page=1&pageSize=5

### Rejected, not clamped: page < 1, pageSize > 100, or from-after-to all return 400.
GET {{host}}/applications?pageSize=1000

### Fetch one.
GET {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}

### Attach a skill. Returns 200, not 201 — and re-sending is a no-op, not an error.
POST {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}/skills
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "skillName": "C#",
  "category": "Language",
  "isRequired": true
}

### Attach a requirement. Requirement text is NOT deduplicated.
POST {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}/requirements
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "text": "5+ years .NET",
  "kind": "Qualification",
  "isMustHave": true
}

### Update. PATCH semantics: only the fields you send are applied — but a field you
### DO send has to be valid. `{"title": ""}` is a 400, not a silent blanking (was A4).
PATCH {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "Interviewing",
  "notes": "Phone screen booked for Thursday"
}

### Unlink a skill. The name travels in the path, so encode it: C# is C%23.
# The shared skills row survives — only the join row goes.
DELETE {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}/skills/C%23

### Remove a requirement. Scoped to the parent: passing another application's id 404s.
DELETE {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}/requirements/{{requirementId}}

### Delete the application. The posting and company deliberately survive.
DELETE {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}

### --- GraphQL, same data layer ---

### Query. Same filters, same validation, same defaults as the REST call above — one
### handler serves both. Enums come back SCREAMING_CASE here and PascalCase over REST.
POST {{host}}/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "{ applications(query: { skill: \"C#\", sort: COMPANY, pageSize: 5 }) { totalCount page pageSize items { id company title status skills } } }"
}

### Mutation — the Phase 2.1 shape: an input object in, a response DTO out.
POST {{host}}/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "mutation($id: UUID!) { addSkillToPosting(applicationId: $id, input: { skillName: \"Terraform\", category: \"Infra\", isRequired: true }) { skillName category isRequired source } }",
  "variables": { "id": "{{applicationId}}" }
}

### A bad id returns NOT_FOUND here and 404 over REST — the same handler decided both.
POST {{host}}/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "mutation { addSkillToPosting(applicationId: \"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\", input: { skillName: \"C#\", isRequired: true }) { skillName } }"
}

### The A4 fix, visible: this used to SUCCEED over GraphQL while returning 400 over REST,
### because the mutation called the repository directly and skipped the endpoint's check.
### The rule lives in CreateApplicationHandler now, so both surfaces reject it.
POST {{host}}/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "mutation { createApplication(input: { company: \"Canva\", title: \"\" }) { id } }"
}

### The A7 fix, visible: no EF entity is in the published schema any more, so the walk
### application -> posting -> company -> postings -> applications -> resumeText that
### reached every resume in the database now fails validation as an unknown field.
GET {{host}}/graphql?sdl

### ------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Phase 2.4 — analytics. Three GROUP BYs, run in Postgres, not in C#.
### Watch the console while these run: each logs a real SQL GROUP BY with the
### LIMIT pushed down.
### ------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Skill demand — the query the whole storage decision was made for. A
### normalized, SHARED skills table turns "what should I learn next?" into one
### GROUP BY; a document store has to read every document and tally in code.
GET {{host}}/stats/skill-demand

### top is capped at 100 and REJECTS out of range rather than clamping, so a
### caller can tell it did not get what it asked for. This one is a 400.
GET {{host}}/stats/skill-demand?top=101

### Status funnel. Every stage comes back, zeros included — "no offers yet" is
### the fact you are looking for, and a stage with no rows cannot come out of a
### GROUP BY, so the empty stages are filled in after the count.
GET {{host}}/stats/funnel

### Roles per company — the rollup `companies` was normalized out for.
GET {{host}}/stats/companies

### All three over GraphQL, in one round trip, off the same slice handlers.
POST {{host}}/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "{ skillDemand(top: 5) { name category postingCount } statusFunnel { total stages { status count } } companyRollup { name applicationCount } }"
}

### The cap is enforced in the handler, so it means the same thing here: INVALID_INPUT.
POST {{host}}/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "{ skillDemand(top: 101) { name } }"
}

### ------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Phase 2.5 — the status lifecycle. One rule, in ApplicationStatusTransitions,
### consulted by the update slice, so REST and GraphQL refuse the same moves with
### the same wording. Run these in order against {{applicationId}}.
### ------------------------------------------------------------------------

### The ordinary path. Applied -> Interviewing.
PATCH {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "Interviewing"
}

### Interviewing -> Offer.
PATCH {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "Offer"
}

### Offer -> Applied. REFUSED, 400: "Cannot move from Offer to Applied."
### An active application does not walk backwards down the pipeline.
PATCH {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "Applied"
}

### The same refusal over GraphQL — same message, under INVALID_INPUT, because
### both surfaces call one handler. This is what "one rule, one implementation"
### looks like from outside the process.
POST {{host}}/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "mutation ($id: UUID!) { updateApplication(id: $id, input: { status: APPLIED }) { id status } }",
  "variables": { "id": "{{applicationId}}" }
}

### A refused transition rolls back the WHOLE patch, not just the status: this
### carries a perfectly legal notes change and writes neither. GET the
### application afterwards and notes is untouched.
PATCH {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "Interviewing",
  "notes": "recruiter called"
}

### Closing it is always allowed.
PATCH {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "Rejected"
}

### Rejected is CLOSED, not terminal — Huntr and Teal both let you move a job
### back out of a closed stage, and so does this. Re-opening succeeds.
PATCH {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "Interviewing"
}

### But an Offer can only be reached from an ACTIVE application. Close it again,
### then try to jump straight to an offer: REFUSED, 400.
PATCH {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "Rejected"
}

###
PATCH {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "Offer"
}

### A patch that never mentions status is never a transition, so it succeeds even
### though the application is closed. This is the case that would make the whole
### feature feel broken if it were wrong.
PATCH {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "notes": "worth trying again in six months"
}
